{
 "id": "shall-we-gather",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Shall We Gather at the River",
 "description": "Robert Lowry's gathering hymn of 1864 in his own four parts, for 2 to 4 singers: the first verse and the chorus, cheerful and steady. The alto sings mostly in thirds under the tune; in the chorus the tenor and bass sing \"Yes, we'll gather\" high, close under the soprano and alto, then everyone settles back for \"That flows by the throne of God\".",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "hymn",
  "gospel"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 90,
 "key": "Eb",
 "timeSignature": "4/4",
 "pickupBeats": 0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Robert Lowry (1864)",
  "tune": "Robert Lowry (1864)",
  "arrangement": "The four parts Robert Lowry printed (1869), adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Lowry died 1899",
  "tune": "public domain: Lowry died 1899",
  "arrangement": "The four parts as Lowry printed them in Bright Jewels (public domain: printed 1866 in Bradbury's New Golden Shower, which names no harmonizer, and 1869 in Bright Jewels; Lowry died 1899, Bradbury 1868), in the printed key: Lowry's tune with the printed alto, tenor and bass, with six two-note syllables in the alto and tenor sung on one note, the alto's last note and four tenor notes changed so the duet ends on a full chord and the inner parts have no wide leaps; breaths written in for beginners",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "Robert Lowry, Bright Jewels for the Sunday School (New York: Biglow & Main, 1869), p. 110, \"Beautiful River\", signed \"R. L.\": the four parts in E-flat, verse 1 and the chorus as printed under the music. University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill Music Library copy, archive.org/details/brightjewelsfors00lowr_0/page/n113 (a second scan of it, brightjewelsfors00lowr_1, agrees). The same setting and words are on p. 25 of William B. Bradbury's The New Golden Shower (1866), \"By permission of the author, Rev. R. Lowry\", which names no harmonizer: archive.org/details/newgoldenshowerc00brad/page/n26. Compared with the earliest known printing, Happy Voices (New York: American Tract Society, 1865), No. 220, p. 153, \"Beautiful River\", \"Rev. R. Lowry\", where the verse is in three parts (no tenor) and the chorus's alto holds E-flat: archive.org/details/happyvoicesnewhy00amer/page/n158"
 },
